  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "SAMPLING-BASED REACHABILITY ANALYSIS",
      "bbox": [
        210.07200622558594,
        41.200374603271484,
        399.2085876464844,
        50.106937408447266
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Appendix D. Computing the Lipschitz constant of a ReLU network from samples",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        93.57196044921875,
        505.22802734375,
        104.3196792602539
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this section, we show that sampling gradients enables obtaining the Lipschitz constant of a neural",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        115.17369842529297,
        522.0001831054688,
        124.92643737792969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "network with ReLU activation functions with high probability. Consider a feed-forward ReLU",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        128.72271728515625,
        522.000244140625,
        138.47544860839844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "neural network f : Rn →Rn with ℓ∈N layers, given as",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        137.52825927734375,
        338.4206237792969,
        152.0254364013672
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "f(x) = W ℓxℓ+ bℓ,",
      "bbox": [
        136.27999877929688,
        162.3677978515625,
        223.03369140625,
        174.51943969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "xk+1 = φk(W kxk + bk), k = 0, . . . , ℓ−1,",
      "bbox": [
        235.76461791992188,
        160.328125,
        428.2176513671875,
        174.51943969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "x0 = x,",
      "bbox": [
        440.9485778808594,
        160.328125,
        475.7276611328125,
        174.51943969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where (W k, bk)ℓ",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        185.6478271484375,
        163.16098022460938,
        197.4944305419922
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "k=0 are the network weights and biases, and each φk : Rnk →Rnk+1 is deﬁned as",
      "bbox": [
        159.7019500732422,
        183.60821533203125,
        521.9993286132812,
        200.8154754638672
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "φk(x) = (ϕ(x1), . . . , ϕ(xnk)) with ϕ(z) = max(0, z). Note that f is piecewise-afﬁne.",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993896484375,
        199.1968994140625,
        469.52862548828125,
        212.88877868652344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Let X ⊂Rn be a non-empty compact set. Let A = {A1, . . . , AN} be the set of all polytopes",
      "bbox": [
        106.93594360351562,
        210.88079833984375,
        522.0028686523438,
        225.47137451171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Ai ⊆X where f|Ai is afﬁne, which we call the activation regions of f. Let ΛN = mini=1,...,N λ(Ai)",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993896484375,
        226.40594482421875,
        520.8025512695312,
        243.08055114746094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "λ(X)",
      "bbox": [
        478.0769958496094,
        235.74740600585938,
        496.7966613769531,
        243.27117919921875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "be the smallest (normalized Lebesgue) volume of all activation regions. Note that f is Lipschitz",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        243.799072265625,
        521.9990234375,
        253.7263641357422
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "continuous over X, since it is continuous and restricted to a compact subset. Thus, for some L ≥0,",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        256.7371826171875,
        522.0003051757812,
        267.2753601074219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "∥f(x1) −f(x2)∥≤L∥x1 −x2∥",
      "bbox": [
        199.09100341796875,
        279.47119140625,
        343.4135437011719,
        291.4063720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "∀x1, x2 ∈X.",
      "bbox": [
        354.3226318359375,
        279.47119140625,
        412.91168212890625,
        291.4063720703125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Further, since f is piecewise-afﬁne, f is L-Lipschitz continuous with",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        302.81707763671875,
        391.5572814941406,
        312.7443542480469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "L =",
      "bbox": [
        209.99795532226562,
        325.55108642578125,
        228.93922424316406,
        335.2383728027344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "max",
      "bbox": [
        238.3319549560547,
        325.55108642578125,
        258.6337890625,
        335.2383728027344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "i=1,...,N{∥∇f(xi)∥for some xi ∈Ai}.",
      "bbox": [
        231.96795654296875,
        324.940185546875,
        402.00469970703125,
        341.9332275390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We propose the following sampling-based method to recover the Lipschitz constant L:",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        353.09210205078125,
        467.1076965332031,
        363.0193786621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "1. Draw M random samples xi in X according to the uniform probability measure over X.",
      "bbox": [
        103.635986328125,
        373.5191955566406,
        502.62322998046875,
        385.693359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2. Evaluate Li = ∥∇f(xi)∥for all i = 1, ..., M.",
      "bbox": [
        103.63595581054688,
        392.6731872558594,
        318.5392150878906,
        404.60736083984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "3. Set ˆL = maxi=1,...,M Li.",
      "bbox": [
        103.63595581054688,
        409.68109130859375,
        226.51022338867188,
        423.8143615722656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In general, with this approach, providing statistical guarantees on whether ˆL is a valid Lipschitz",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993896484375,
        431.7370910644531,
        522.0044555664062,
        444.4213562011719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "constant for f is challenging; the analysis would rely on the Hessian of f which is a-priori unknown.",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        448.0440673828125,
        521.9983520507812,
        457.9713439941406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In this speciﬁc setting, f is piecewise-afﬁne, which we leverage in the analysis below.",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993896484375,
        461.59307861328125,
        464.1826171875,
        471.5203552246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Lemma 7 With the previous notations, deﬁne δM = N(1 −ΛN)M. Then,",
      "bbox": [
        89.99993133544922,
        480.71173095703125,
        414.6335144042969,
        494.00634765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "P(f is ˆL-Lipschitz continuous over X) ≥1 −δM.",
      "bbox": [
        195.94894409179688,
        502.6070556640625,
        416.05267333984375,
        516.170166015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Proof Since L = maxi=1,...,N{∥∇f(xi)∥for some xi ∈Ai}, a sufﬁcient condition for f to be",
      "bbox": [
        89.99996948242188,
        527.4881591796875,
        521.998779296875,
        539.4234008789062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ˆL-Lipschitz continuous is that at least one point xj was sampled in each region Ai. Thus,",
