    "text": "SAMPLING-BASED REACHABILITY ANALYSIS \n\n# **Appendix D. Computing the Lipschitz constant of a ReLU network from samples** \n\nIn this section, we show that sampling gradients enables obtaining the Lipschitz constant of a neural network with ReLU activation functions with high probability. Consider a feed-forward ReLU neural network _f_ : R<sup>_n_</sup> _→_ R<sup>_n_</sup> with _ℓ ∈_ N layers, given as \n\n\n\nwhere ( _W_<sup>_k_</sup> _, b_<sup>_k_</sup> )<sup>_ℓ_</sup> _k_ =0<sup>are the network weights and biases, and each</sup><sup>_φk_:R</sup><sup>_nk→_R</sup><sup>_nk_+1is defined as</sup> _φ_<sup>_k_</sup> ( _x_ ) = ( _ϕ_ ( _x_ 1) _, . . . , ϕ_ ( _xnk_ )) with _ϕ_ ( _z_ ) = max(0 _, z_ ). Note that _f_ is piecewise-affine. \n\nLet _X ⊂_ R<sup>_n_</sup> be a non-empty compact set. Let _A_ = _{A_ 1 _, . . . , AN }_ be the set of all polytopes _Ai ⊆X_ where _f |Ai_ is affine, which we call the activation regions of _f_ . Let Λ _N_ =<sup>min</sup><sup>_i_=1</sup> _λ_<sup>_<u>,...</u>_</sup> ( _X_<sup>_<u>,N</u>_</sup> )<sup>_λ_</sup><sup><u>(</u></sup><sup>_Ai_</sup><sup><u>)</u></sup> be the smallest (normalized Lebesgue) volume of all activation regions. Note that _f_ is Lipschitz continuous over _X_ , since it is continuous and restricted to a compact subset. Thus, for some _L ≥_ 0, \n\n\n\nFurther, since _f_ is piecewise-affine, _f_ is _L_ -Lipschitz continuous with \n\n\n\nWe propose the following sampling-based method to recover the Lipschitz constant _L_ : \n\n1. Draw _M_ random samples _xi_ in _X_ according to the uniform probability measure over _X_ . \n\n2. Evaluate _Li_ = _∥∇f_ ( _xi_ ) _∥_ for all _i_ = 1 _,_ ... _, M_ . \n\n3. Set _L_<sup>ˆ</sup> = max _i_ =1 _,...,M Li_ . \n\nIn general, with this approach, providing statistical guarantees on whether _L_<sup>ˆ</sup> is a valid Lipschitz constant for _f_ is challenging; the analysis would rely on the Hessian of _f_ which is a-priori unknown. In this specific setting, _f_ is piecewise-aff